{
  "title": "Sub sessions: agents hand tasks to local and remote sessions they own",
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "outcome",
      "title": "What changes",
      "purpose": "State the capability in one reading so every later section can be checked against it.",
      "collapsed": false,
      "kind": "markdown",
      "body": "Any agent, a team member included, can hand a task to a sub session it owns: a session that does that one task, reports back to its owner, and is closed when the task is done. A sub session runs locally or on a remote agent node such as brchen-db3, where UI-heavy work such as validating VS Code builds, reproducing bugs, or checking sites runs without competing for this machine's screen, mouse, or keyboard.\n\nEvery sub session is a Worker owned by its agent's session. One registry records which agent owns which sub sessions, one toolset creates, messages, lists, and closes them wherever they run, and Agent Teams shows each sub session's transcript and the sub sessions under each agent.\n\nThe owning agent is the expert. It gives each sub session a complete brief, reviews what comes back against the brief's acceptance criteria before using or reporting it, and closes the sub session afterwards. Sub sessions are not team members: only their owner messages them, and the user can open and watch any of them.\n\nRemote sub sessions run in VS Code Insiders' standalone Agent Host on each node, which a remote provider beside Copilot, Codex, and Claude reaches over the Agent Host Protocol (AHP) through a private dev tunnel. BryanBot and VS Code use the same nodes over AHP directly. Workflows such as owning a pull request through to merge are skills layered on top.\n\nWhat stays the same: service connectors such as BryanBot keep connector protocol v1, and agents that never delegate run nothing new."
    },
    {
      "id": "not-in-scope",
      "title": "Not doing",
      "purpose": "Close tempting extensions so adding any of them becomes a decision rather than drift.",
      "collapsed": false,
      "kind": "list",
      "style": "bullet",
      "items": [
        "Sub sessions owning sub sessions; each has exactly one owner, the agent that created it.",
        "Other agents or connectors messaging a sub session; they ask its owner.",
        "Progress in the team thread between reports; the owner hears each finished turn, and the user can open the transcript.",
        "Forwarding a remote session's tool approvals to the user; remote sessions approve their own tool calls on the node.",
        "BryanBot reaching nodes through Agent Teams; it talks to nodes over AHP directly.",
        "A separate scheduler service; placement stays a pure module."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "findings",
      "title": "What the design builds on",
      "purpose": "Ground the shape in capabilities that already exist, so the pool extends them instead of rebuilding them.",
      "collapsed": true,
      "kind": "findings",
      "sourcePolicy": "reference",
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "f-standalone-host",
          "statement": "VS Code's CLI runs a standalone Agent Host with no editor window (`code agent host` with `--port`, `--connection-token-file`, and `--idle-timeout`), and Insiders 1.147 already ships it.",
          "whyItMatters": "Nodes need no running VS Code window, and sessions, multiple clients, reconnects, approvals, and three providers come with the host.",
          "sources": [
            "microsoft/vscode cli/src/commands/args.rs#AgentHostArgs",
            "`code-insiders agent --help` on brchen-db4, 1.147.0-insider"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-host-updates",
          "statement": "The CLI downloads new agent host server builds every 6 hours and starts them once no session or client is active; the CLI binary itself updates only when a client asks.",
          "whyItMatters": "Node maintenance only has to update the CLI and restart the host while idle.",
          "sources": [
            "microsoft/vscode cli/src/tunnels/agent_host.rs#run_update_loop",
            "microsoft/vscode cli/src/tunnels/control_server.rs#handle_update"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-tunnel-host",
          "statement": "brchen-db3 already runs a supervised Insiders tunnel host from a logon task, with a dedicated CLI and data directory.",
          "sources": [
            "vscode-space .github/scripts/tunnel-host/Install-TunnelHost.ps1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-client-ids",
          "statement": "AHP clients choose session URIs and turn IDs, and creating a session that already exists fails with `SessionAlreadyExists`.",
          "whyItMatters": "Retries can be made safe without a separate idempotency store.",
          "sources": [
            "microsoft/vscode src/vs/platform/agentHost/common/state/protocol/channels-session/commands.ts#CreateSessionParams",
            "microsoft/vscode src/vs/platform/agentHost/common/state/protocol/channels-chat/actions.ts#ChatTurnStartedAction"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-turn-meta",
          "statement": "The host drops a turn's `_meta` when the turn starts, so it cannot keep a client's tag for a turn.",
          "sources": [
            "microsoft/vscode src/vs/platform/agentHost/common/state/protocol/channels-chat/reducer.ts (ChatTurnStarted case)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-ahp-replay",
          "statement": "A fresh connection's chat snapshot holds a session's completed turns with every response part, including tool inputs, results, and shell output previews, so a turn projected live from chat actions and the same turn replayed from history build the same Agent Teams transcript.",
          "whyItMatters": "A remote provider can serve history from the node without keeping its own copy.",
          "sources": [
            "Provider probe against Insiders agent host 944d79c on brchen-db3, 2026-11-15"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-ahp-client-tools",
          "statement": "The host asks the owning client to confirm each call to a tool that client contributed, then waits for its result, so Agent Teams' guard reviews and screens its own tools on a node as it does locally.",
          "sources": [
            "Provider probe against Insiders agent host 944d79c on brchen-db3, 2026-11-15"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-ahp-approvals",
          "statement": "In autopilot with the host's `default` approval mode, Copilot runs reads such as `Get-Date` without asking and holds writes such as `New-Item` for a client's confirmation. A client's denial cancels the call, but the model reads only the host's fixed \"The user rejected this tool call\" text, whatever reason or suggestion the client sends. No approval mode holds every call, and a client can't rewrite a native tool's result.",
          "whyItMatters": "Security can refuse only the native calls the host holds, and can't mark untrusted content that a node's agent reads.",
          "sources": [
            "Provider probe against Insiders agent host 944d79c on brchen-db3, 2026-11-15",
            "resolveSessionConfig on brchen-db3: availableApprovalModes default, assisted, autoApprove"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-ahp-holds",
          "statement": "In autopilot with the `default` approval mode, brchen-db3's host held every external write tried (`gh api -X POST`, `gh pr comment`, `git push`, `Invoke-RestMethod -Method Post`) and every web read (`web_fetch`, `curl`, `Invoke-WebRequest`) for a client's confirmation, but ran GitHub reads (`gh issue view`, the GitHub MCP server's `issue_read`) and `git ls-remote` without asking. The session config's `permissions` rules changed none of this: `ask` rules for `Shell(*)` and `Shell(gh *)`, and a `deny` rule for `Mcp(github-mcp-server/issue_read)`, were accepted and ignored.",
          "whyItMatters": "Agent Teams can refuse any external write a remote session makes, but issue and pull request text, the main injection source for PR work, reaches the remote model unreviewed.",
          "sources": [
            "Approval probe against Insiders agent host 944d79c on brchen-db3, 2026-11-15"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-agent-sandbox",
          "statement": "The host's per-session `sandboxEnabled` switch didn't help on brchen-db3: it refused to start every shell command until the machine allows the sandbox's local proxy, it marks sandboxed commands as needing no confirmation, and the host's own GitHub MCP reads run outside it. A client can toggle or stop a session's MCP servers.",
          "whyItMatters": "Neither the sandbox nor permission rules remove a session's GitHub read path; the node's environment has to.",
          "sources": [
            "Sandbox probe against Insiders agent host 944d79c on brchen-db3, 2026-11-15",
            "@microsoft/agent-host-protocol 1.0.0 channels-session/actions.d.ts#SessionCustomizationToggledAction"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-vscode-tunnel-ahp",
          "statement": "`code tunnel` (also `code agent host --tunnel`) forwards the machine's agent host on tunnel port 31546 beside the editor's 31545, injecting the host's connection token itself. With the owner's gh CLI token, the Dev Tunnels API lists the owner's VS Code tunnels by machine-name label and issues a connect token, and Bun's WebSocket with that token reached brchen-db3's agent host through port 31546 and negotiated AHP 1.0.0. The port denies anonymous access.",
          "whyItMatters": "A node needs only its VS Code tunnel: no second dev tunnel, standalone host, token file, or dev tunnel CLI sign-in.",
          "sources": [
            "microsoft/vscode cli/src/constants.rs#AGENT_HOST_PORT",
            "microsoft/vscode cli/src/tunnels/agent_host.rs (forwarded agent-host tunnel port)",
            "Probe from brchen-db4 to tunnel sneaky-book-12dc72v (brchen-db3), 2026-11-15"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-host-seams",
          "statement": "AHP lets a client add tools to a session as one of several active clients, steer a running turn, read and write node files, and see MCP sign-in as a session input request.",
          "sources": [
            "microsoft/vscode src/vs/platform/agentHost/common/state/protocol/channels-session/state.ts#SessionActiveClient",
            "microsoft/vscode src/vs/platform/agentHost/common/state/protocol/channels-chat/state.ts#PendingMessageKind",
            "microsoft/vscode src/vs/platform/agentHost/common/state/protocol/common/commands.ts (resourceRead, resourceWrite)",
            "microsoft/vscode src/vs/platform/agentHost/common/state/protocol/channels-session/state.ts#SessionToolAuthenticationRequest"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-connector-seam",
          "statement": "Only the connector link speaks protocol v1; the registry, outbox, status, dev tunnel endpoint, mention routing, and conversation-to-thread map do not depend on it.",
          "sources": [
            "toy-box src/features/connectors/server/link.ts#ConnectorLink",
            "toy-box src/features/connectors/server/index.ts#startLink",
            "toy-box src/features/connectors/server/devtunnel.ts#tunnelEndpoint",
            "toy-box src/features/teams/server/connectors.ts#applyConnectorEvent"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-lead-only",
          "statement": "An unmentioned connector reply wakes only the lead, and the transcript recomputes each stored message's addressee with the same audience policy.",
          "whyItMatters": "A member that asks a connector is never woken by the answer, and any routing input must be stored with the message so every reader agrees.",
          "sources": [
            "toy-box src/features/teams/model/index.ts#resolveTeamAudience",
            "toy-box src/features/teams/components/pane/transcript/transcriptRows.ts#addresseeOf"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-bryanbot-ahp",
          "statement": "BryanBot already drives AHP sessions over a plain WebSocket and treats a final answer of `NO_UPDATE` as a silent turn.",
          "sources": [
            "bryan-bot src/server/ahp.ts#connectAhpTransport",
            "bryan-bot src/server/connector.ts#agentTeamsTurnPrompt"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-vscode-space-isolation",
          "statement": "vscode-space tells agents to isolate child-repo changes in `<repo>.worktrees/<task-slug>` and to commit root changes directly to main.",
          "sources": [
            "vscode-space .github/copilot-instructions.md"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-rdp-lock",
          "statement": "Closing a Remote Desktop window locks the session and breaks UI automation; moving the session to the console with `tscon` keeps it unlocked.",
          "sources": [
            "https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/devops/pipelines/test/ui-testing-considerations"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-personal-skills",
          "statement": "Copilot loads personal skills from `~/.copilot/skills` in every workspace.",
          "sources": [
            "https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/how-tos/copilot-cli/customize-copilot/add-skills"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-host-auth",
          "statement": "A standalone agent host's Copilot provider requires the client to `authenticate` `https://api.github.com` with a GitHub token before it runs turns; the owner's gh CLI token works.",
          "whyItMatters": "The pool, not the node, supplies the owner's credential to each node connection.",
          "sources": [
            "Probe against Insiders agent host 944d79c on brchen-db4, 2026-11-13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-create-conflict",
          "statement": "Creating a session whose URI already exists is rejected with `-32002` (\"Conflicting chat storage identity\"), not the documented `-32003`.",
          "whyItMatters": "A retried create is detected by subscribing to the session URI, not by matching an error code.",
          "sources": [
            "Probe against Insiders agent host 944d79c on brchen-db4, 2026-11-13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-active-client-subscription",
          "statement": "A client's active-client role and tools last only while it holds a subscription to the session; unsubscribing removes them, while a 20-second disconnect keeps them and a pending call to the client's tool, which completes after reconnecting with the same client ID.",
          "whyItMatters": "The pool keeps every open remote session subscribed, under one stable client ID.",
          "sources": [
            "Probe against Insiders agent host 944d79c on brchen-db4, 2026-11-13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-background-work",
          "statement": "An autopilot turn can end while its shell keeps running as background work; that work's completion reaches the agent later as a system notification.",
          "whyItMatters": "A finished turn does not mean the session is idle.",
          "sources": [
            "Probe against Insiders agent host 944d79c on brchen-db4, 2026-11-13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-workers",
          "statement": "Workers already records session-owned Workers with their parent session before they run, deletes them with their parent, and nests them under it in the sidebar. Standard and Hyper sessions get `spawn_worker`, `wait_for_sessions`, `deliver_message`, and `delete_session`; team agents get none of them.",
          "sources": [
            "toy-box src/features/workers/AGENTS.md",
            "toy-box src/features/workers/server/tools.ts#spawnWorkerTool",
            "toy-box src/features/teams/server/agent.ts#getTeamAgentConfiguration"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-delegation-practice",
          "statement": "Published practice: the delegating agent keeps ownership of the answer and calls specialists as bounded tools (OpenAI Agents SDK); each delegated task needs an objective, an output format, guidance on tools and sources, and clear boundaries, with effort limits so an orchestrator doesn't spawn dozens of workers (Anthropic); workers given only a summary make conflicting implicit decisions, so share full context and keep parallel workers off shared artifacts (Cognition); and missing or superficial verification is a leading cause of multi-agent failure (MAST).",
          "sources": [
            "https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/agents/orchestration",
            "https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system",
            "https://cognition.com/blog/dont-build-multi-agents",
            "https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.13657"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "f-native-subagents",
          "statement": "Copilot, Claude, and Codex each run their own subagents inside a turn, and Agent Teams already projects them as agent cards in the parent's transcript. They end with the parent's turn, run in its process on the same machine, take no follow-ups, and belong to one provider.",
          "sources": [
            "toy-box src/features/providers/server/copilot/AGENTS.md",
            "toy-box src/features/providers/server/claude/AGENTS.md",
            "toy-box src/features/providers/server/codex/AGENTS.md"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "flow",
      "title": "How a sub session is created, reports, and closes",
      "purpose": "Show the one topology every requirement below serves, and where each step runs.",
      "collapsed": false,
      "kind": "exhibits",
      "sourcePolicy": "optional",
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "x-sub-flow",
          "title": "A sub session's task, from brief to close, with a human-needed detour",
          "change": "new",
          "description": "Every report goes to the owning agent, which reviews it before anything reaches the team or the user.",
          "kind": "flow",
          "nodes": [
            {
              "id": "brief",
              "title": "Agent creates a sub session with a brief"
            },
            {
              "id": "register",
              "title": "Workers records the agent as owner"
            },
            {
              "id": "place",
              "title": "Placement picks this machine or a node with capacity"
            },
            {
              "id": "run",
              "title": "The sub session runs a turn"
            },
            {
              "id": "ui",
              "title": "`ui-slot` grants UI capacity on a node"
            },
            {
              "id": "report",
              "title": "The turn ends with an answer and evidence"
            },
            {
              "id": "review",
              "title": "The owner wakes and reviews it against the brief"
            },
            {
              "id": "close",
              "title": "The owner follows up, or closes the sub session"
            },
            {
              "id": "human",
              "title": "The sub session needs a human"
            },
            {
              "id": "user",
              "title": "The user acts on the node and answers"
            },
            {
              "id": "resume",
              "title": "The sub session resumes and checks the step worked"
            }
          ],
          "connections": [
            {
              "id": "c-register",
              "from": "brief",
              "to": "register",
              "label": "is recorded by"
            },
            {
              "id": "c-place",
              "from": "register",
              "to": "place",
              "label": "is placed by"
            },
            {
              "id": "c-run",
              "from": "place",
              "to": "run",
              "label": "starts"
            },
            {
              "id": "c-ui",
              "from": "run",
              "to": "ui",
              "label": "claims UI capacity from"
            },
            {
              "id": "c-report",
              "from": "run",
              "to": "report",
              "label": "ends with"
            },
            {
              "id": "c-review",
              "from": "report",
              "to": "review",
              "label": "wakes"
            },
            {
              "id": "c-close",
              "from": "review",
              "to": "close",
              "label": "leads to"
            },
            {
              "id": "c-human",
              "from": "run",
              "to": "human",
              "label": "may stop at"
            },
            {
              "id": "c-user",
              "from": "human",
              "to": "user",
              "label": "asks"
            },
            {
              "id": "c-resume",
              "from": "user",
              "to": "resume",
              "label": "lets"
            }
          ],
          "paths": [
            {
              "id": "p-task",
              "title": "A task from brief to close",
              "purpose": "Show that every report reaches the owner, who reviews it before anything else happens.",
              "start": "brief",
              "connectionIds": [
                "c-register",
                "c-place",
                "c-run",
                "c-report",
                "c-review",
                "c-close"
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p-ui",
              "title": "UI work on a node",
              "purpose": "Show where a remote sub session gets its share of the node's desktop.",
              "start": "run",
              "connectionIds": [
                "c-ui"
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p-human",
              "title": "A human-needed detour",
              "purpose": "Show that a sub session waits for the user and checks the step before carrying on.",
              "start": "run",
              "connectionIds": [
                "c-human",
                "c-user",
                "c-resume"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "reply-routing",
      "title": "Replies wake the agent who asked",
      "purpose": "Fix connector routing first: it is general, small, and every pool result depends on it.",
      "collapsed": false,
      "kind": "records",
      "view": "table",
      "sourcePolicy": "reference",
      "subject": "Part",
      "fields": [
        {
          "id": "behavior",
          "label": "Behavior",
          "kind": "text"
        }
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "r-reply-to-wire",
          "subject": "Connector event",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "An event may carry `replyTo`, the ID of the team message it answers. It is optional, so connectors that omit it keep today's routing."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "r-asked-by",
          "subject": "Stored reply",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "Teams resolves `replyTo` to a message in the same team and stores that message's agent author as the reply's `askedBy`. A user's message, an unknown ID, or another team's message stores nothing."
          },
          "basedOn": [
            "f-lead-only"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "r-audience",
          "subject": "Audience policy",
          "change": "modified",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "An unmentioned connector message with `askedBy` wakes that agent while it is on the team, and the lead otherwise. Leading mentions still decide when present. Delivery and the transcript's addressee read the same stored input."
          },
          "source": "toy-box src/features/teams/model/index.ts#resolveTeamAudience",
          "basedOn": [
            "f-lead-only"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "r-guidance",
          "subject": "Agent guidance",
          "change": "modified",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "Agents learn that a connector's reply wakes whoever asked, so they end their turn after asking rather than waiting for it."
          },
          "source": "toy-box src/features/teams/server/tools.ts"
        },
        {
          "id": "r-bryanbot-reply-to",
          "subject": "BryanBot",
          "change": "modified",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "BryanBot sets `replyTo` to the Agent Teams message each answer responds to."
          },
          "source": "bryan-bot src/server/connector.ts"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sub-sessions",
      "title": "Sub sessions",
      "purpose": "Let any agent own sessions that each do one task for it, wherever they run.",
      "collapsed": false,
      "kind": "records",
      "view": "cards",
      "sourcePolicy": "reference",
      "subject": "Capability",
      "fields": [
        {
          "id": "behavior",
          "label": "Behavior",
          "kind": "text"
        }
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "r-sub-owner",
          "subject": "Sub session",
          "change": "modified",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "A sub session is a Worker owned by its agent's session. Workers records the owner before the sub session runs and removes the record when it is deleted, and deleting the agent's session deletes its sub sessions."
          },
          "source": "toy-box src/features/workers/AGENTS.md",
          "basedOn": [
            "f-workers"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "r-sub-tools",
          "subject": "One toolset",
          "change": "modified",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "Every agent except a sub session, team agents included, shares one toolset in place of `spawn_worker`: `create_sub_session(name, brief, location)`, `message_sub_session(id, message)`, `list_sub_sessions()`, and `close_sub_session(id)`. Location is this machine or a pool. Each acts only on the caller's own sub sessions."
          },
          "source": "toy-box src/features/workers/server/tools.ts#spawnWorkerTool",
          "basedOn": [
            "f-workers"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "r-sub-brief",
          "subject": "Brief",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "Creating a sub session takes a brief: goal, context (findings, decisions made, references, and attached files), deliverable, acceptance criteria, boundaries, and how to report. Fixed code renders it as the first message with a note that names the owner, says results return to it automatically, and asks the sub session to ask rather than guess when the brief conflicts with what it finds."
          },
          "basedOn": [
            "f-delegation-practice"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "r-sub-results",
          "subject": "Reports",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "Each execution a sub session finishes, whoever prompted it, wakes the owner at once with its final answer or error. The owner checks it against the brief's acceptance criteria before using or reporting it. An answer of exactly `NO_UPDATE`, or none, wakes no one unless the execution made external writes, and an aborted run reports nothing. A sub session whose background work still runs stays busy after its turn ends, and reports when that work ends."
          },
          "basedOn": [
            "f-delegation-practice",
            "f-bryanbot-ahp",
            "f-background-work"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "r-outcome-review",
          "subject": "Outcome review",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "Each report, local or remote, lists the external writes its sub session ran since the previous report, as Security classifies them when they run: pushes, pull request and issue changes, GitHub API writes, web posts, and publishes; closing lists any no report did. The owner checks the report, its artifacts, and each of those writes against the brief's scope and what it knows, looking at the resulting pull requests, issues, comments, and pages itself rather than trusting the report, and undoes or raises anything out of scope."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "r-sub-wakes",
          "subject": "Wakes",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "A sub session waiting on something slow, such as CI or a review, calls `wake_after(minutes, reason)`. Agent Teams starts that turn when it is due, counting it against the node's compute slots for a remote one; any newer message cancels it, and overdue wakes run at startup."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "r-sub-limits",
          "subject": "Limits",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "An agent owns at most 5 open sub sessions, and a sub session can't create sub sessions of its own."
          },
          "basedOn": [
            "f-delegation-practice"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "r-sub-access",
          "subject": "Access",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "Only the owner messages a sub session. Other agents and connectors can't address it, and it never joins the team's conversation. The user can open, watch, and steer any sub session."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "r-sub-view",
          "subject": "View",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "The team members table lists each agent's open sub sessions with their task and live state, and opens one to show its reports; the sidebar nests a standard session's under it."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "r-sub-cleanup",
          "subject": "Closing",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "Closing a sub session stops and deletes it, archiving a remote one on its node; an owner that wants a wrap-up asks for it first. A sub session left idle for a day after reporting, with no wake of its own pending, reminds its owner once, and deleting an agent or its team closes its sub sessions."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "r-sub-guidance",
          "subject": "When to delegate",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "Agent instructions say to use the provider's own subagents for quick work within a turn, such as a parallel search or a diff review, and a sub session for a task that outlives the turn, needs another machine or the UI, or needs its own review."
          },
          "basedOn": [
            "f-native-subagents",
            "f-delegation-practice"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "remote",
      "title": "Remote sub sessions",
      "purpose": "Run sub sessions on agent nodes as ordinary Agent Teams sessions.",
      "collapsed": false,
      "kind": "records",
      "view": "cards",
      "sourcePolicy": "reference",
      "subject": "Capability",
      "fields": [
        {
          "id": "behavior",
          "label": "Behavior",
          "kind": "text"
        }
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "r-remote-provider",
          "subject": "Remote provider",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "A remote provider implements the Providers contract over AHP. A sub session placed on a node is a Session that this provider runs in the node's Agent Host, projecting its turns into session events, so Agent Teams shows its transcript and history like any other session's."
          },
          "source": "toy-box src/features/providers/server/provider.ts",
          "basedOn": [
            "f-bryanbot-ahp",
            "f-turn-meta"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "r-remote-nodes",
          "subject": "Pool connector",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "A pool connector, added and edited in the connector dialog, lists its nodes: machine name, workspaces as repository → folder pairs, scratch folder, compute slots, and a draining flag. Its link keeps one AHP connection per node through the owner's VS Code tunnel for that machine, under a stable client ID, which the remote provider runs sub sessions over. Agent Teams stores no secrets: with the owner's GitHub token from the gh CLI it finds the tunnel by machine name, gets a connect token from the Dev Tunnels service, and signs the host in."
          },
          "source": "toy-box src/features/connectors/server/pool/link.ts",
          "basedOn": [
            "f-host-auth",
            "f-vscode-tunnel-ahp"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "r-pool-location",
          "subject": "A place, not a conversation",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "A pool connector is where sub sessions run, not something a team talks to: mentions don't address it, and `create_sub_session` names a pool, or one of its nodes, as its location. `read_team` lists pools with their nodes' state, and the team members table shows each remote sub session's node."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "r-placement",
          "subject": "Placement",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "A sub session created for a node is placed on a reachable, non-draining node with a free compute slot that lists the team workspace's repository, starting in that folder; with no such node, it starts in a qualifying node's scratch folder. Requests wait in arrival order when no node qualifies."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "r-idempotent",
          "subject": "Safe retries",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "A remote session's URI derives from its Worker's session ID, and turn IDs from message IDs. Before creating a session the provider subscribes to its URI, so a retried create or send never duplicates work, and each finished turn is reported once."
          },
          "basedOn": [
            "f-client-ids",
            "f-create-conflict"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "r-remote-subscription",
          "subject": "Live session",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "The provider keeps each open remote sub session subscribed under its node's client ID, so its tools stay registered. A message starts its next turn or steers the running one, and stopping cancels the running turn."
          },
          "basedOn": [
            "f-active-client-subscription"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "r-remote-tools",
          "subject": "Node tools",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "Remote sub sessions receive their Agent Teams tools as an AHP active client, including `wake_after`, `set_status`, and `request_human(key, what, steps, verify)`."
          },
          "basedOn": [
            "f-host-seams"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "r-files-in",
          "subject": "Files in",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "Files attached to a brief or a message are written to the node and attached to the turn."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "r-human-needed",
          "subject": "Human needed",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "`request_human`, and host MCP sign-in requests, mark the sub session Input needed and tell its owner and the user once per key and node, naming the node, the need, the waiting sub sessions, the steps, and a warning never to paste secrets. The user's answer resumes every sub session waiting on that key, which then checks the step worked."
          },
          "basedOn": [
            "f-host-seams"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "r-status",
          "subject": "Status",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "Each node's connection and its compute and UI slot use show in Settings and beside its sub sessions."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "r-files-out",
          "subject": "Files out",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "Later: a remote session's screenshots return as thread attachments, and its videos as links."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "r-node-sandbox",
          "subject": "Sandbox and node auditor",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "Later: a remote session runs sandboxed on its node without its own path to outside content, and an auditor on the same node screens what it reads, so large content is judged where it lives instead of crossing the tunnel."
          },
          "basedOn": [
            "f-agent-sandbox",
            "f-ahp-holds"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "node-kit",
      "title": "Node kit in vscode-space",
      "purpose": "Define what every node provides to every session on it, whoever started the session.",
      "collapsed": false,
      "kind": "records",
      "view": "cards",
      "sourcePolicy": "reference",
      "subject": "Piece",
      "fields": [
        {
          "id": "behavior",
          "label": "Behavior",
          "kind": "text"
        }
      ],
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "r-node-host",
          "subject": "Tunnel host task",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "The `VS Code Tunnel Host` logon task in the owner's desktop session runs a supervisor that starts `code-insiders tunnel --name <machine>` from a standalone CLI under `~/.vscode-tunnel-host`. That one owner-only tunnel carries the remote editor and the machine's agent host, and the supervisor restarts it when it exits. The agent-node kit adds only UI work to it: `ui-slot`, the RDP hold, and the return to the console."
          },
          "source": "vscode-space .github/scripts/tunnel-host/Install-TunnelHost.ps1",
          "basedOn": [
            "f-standalone-host",
            "f-host-updates",
            "f-vscode-tunnel-ahp"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "r-node-maintenance",
          "subject": "Idle maintenance",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "The Tunnel Host supervisor updates the standalone CLI before each start. Every 30 minutes its maintenance task checks for a CLI update; when one is waiting and no agent session has a turn running or holds UI slots, it restarts the tunnel so the update applies. The `repo-sync` task keeps the vscode-space checkout current, and the kit and skill run straight from it, so a pull updates them."
          },
          "source": "vscode-space .github/scripts/tunnel-host/Install-TunnelHost.ps1",
          "basedOn": [
            "f-host-updates"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "r-ui-slot",
          "subject": "`ui-slot`",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "`ui-slot run -- <command>` holds one of the node's UI slots (default 5) while the command runs. `ui-slot hold --desktop` holds all of them, for real input or screen recording, until `ui-slot release` or a time limit. Slots belong to processes, so a crash frees them. A status file lets the pool read current use."
          },
          "basedOn": [
            "f-turn-meta"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "r-rdp-hold",
          "subject": "RDP hold",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "Connecting over RDP takes a human hold that pauses desktop holds while Playwright work continues. Disconnecting releases it and moves the session to the console with `tscon`, so it stays unlocked. A Return to console shortcut on the desktop runs the same command."
          },
          "basedOn": [
            "f-rdp-lock"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "r-setup-record",
          "subject": "Setup record",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "Sessions record confirmed human setup (key, account, browser profile or tool, time, session; never secrets) in `~/.agent-node/human-setup.json`, and check it before asking. Sign-in-only sites keep one signed-in browser profile per node, which each session copies into its own profile."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "r-node-skill",
          "subject": "`agent-node` skill",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "A personal skill in `~/.copilot/skills` documents `ui-slot`, human-needed requests, and the setup record for every session on the node."
          },
          "basedOn": [
            "f-personal-skills"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "r-node-setup-script",
          "subject": "Setup script",
          "change": "new",
          "values": {
            "behavior": "One script installs the host task, tunnel, kit, skill, RDP tasks, gh, and power settings, so adding a node is one run."
          },
          "basedOn": [
            "f-tunnel-host"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pr-workflow",
      "title": "PR workflow skill",
      "purpose": "Show that the full PR lifecycle is a workflow on top of the pool, owned by the workspace it serves.",
      "collapsed": false,
      "kind": "markdown",
      "body": "A `remote-pr` skill in vscode-space builds on its `github-pr`, `worktree-isolation`, and `demo-capture` skills. The requesting agent and the remote session both read it.\n\n**The handoff** is a new top-level `@pool` message carrying:\n\n- the goal and deliverable: a merged fix, a draft for review, or an investigation only;\n- the analysis so far, with evidence attached as files;\n- the suspected fix location as repo, path, and symbol at a commit SHA, never local paths;\n- acceptance criteria and required validation, including UI scenarios and whether video evidence is needed;\n- the PR's shape: linked issue, reviewers, labels;\n- the merge rule: drive to merge, stop at approval, or the conditions for closing.\n\nThe requester hands over the whole task and does not also work on it locally.\n\n**The remote session's stages** each end the turn, so waiting holds no slot:\n\n1. Start: update the vscode-space root, create a worktree from the latest upstream, and ask only if its findings contradict the handoff.\n2. Build: implement, build, and test.\n3. Validate: run UI work through `ui-slot`, and capture evidence with demo-capture.\n4. Draft PR: commit, post the PR link and evidence, `set_status`, then `wake_after`.\n5. Green: fix CI and address Copilot code review threads; wakes that find nothing new end with `NO_UPDATE`; mark the PR ready and request reviewers.\n6. Approval: wake every few hours, and ask the requester only for decisions or stalled reviews.\n7. Merge, or close only with the requester's confirmation unless the handoff allows it.\n8. Clean up: remove the worktree and branch, post the outcome, and call `finish`.\n\nRemote sessions may commit and push learnings and task logs to the vscode-space root on their node. They stage only their own files and resolve conflicts themselves."
    },
    {
      "id": "decisions",
      "title": "Decisions",
      "purpose": "Keep the owner's choices, with the alternatives they rejected, so later changes revisit them deliberately.",
      "collapsed": false,
      "kind": "decisions",
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "d-runtime",
          "question": "What runs remote sessions?",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "o-ahp",
              "label": "VS Code's standalone Agent Host, over AHP",
              "rationale": "Sessions, multiple clients, reconnects, approvals, providers, and server updates already exist and are used daily.",
              "tradeoff": "The protocol moves quickly in Insiders, so a new version can need a client update.",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-sdk-broker",
              "label": "A custom Copilot SDK broker",
              "tradeoff": "Rebuilds what AHP has, is Copilot-only, and leaves every SDK update to us.",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-launcher",
              "label": "A process launcher",
              "tradeoff": "Session management becomes SSH plus tmux rebuilt by hand, and it knows nothing about agents.",
              "adds": []
            }
          ],
          "choice": {
            "optionId": "o-ahp",
            "status": "decided"
          },
          "dependsOn": [],
          "affects": [],
          "basedOn": [
            "f-standalone-host",
            "f-host-updates"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-path",
          "question": "How do agents reach nodes?",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "o-remote-provider",
              "label": "A remote provider speaking AHP, so remote sub sessions are ordinary Sessions",
              "rationale": "One registry, toolset, and transcript view for local and remote sub sessions.",
              "tradeoff": "Agent Teams needs a fourth provider implementation.",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-pool-connector",
              "label": "A pool connector speaking AHP directly",
              "tradeoff": "A second registry beside Workers, and no remote transcripts in Agent Teams.",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-bryanbot",
              "label": "Through @bryanbot",
              "tradeoff": "Every dispatch costs a BryanBot agent turn and more messages.",
              "adds": []
            }
          ],
          "choice": {
            "optionId": "o-remote-provider",
            "status": "decided"
          },
          "dependsOn": [],
          "affects": []
        },
        {
          "id": "d-quality",
          "question": "Which VS Code quality do nodes run?",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "o-insiders",
              "label": "Insiders",
              "rationale": "It matches daily use and gets fixes sooner.",
              "tradeoff": "New protocol versions occasionally need approval.",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-stable",
              "label": "Stable",
              "tradeoff": "Agent Host fixes arrive weeks later.",
              "adds": []
            }
          ],
          "choice": {
            "optionId": "o-insiders",
            "status": "decided"
          },
          "dependsOn": [],
          "affects": []
        },
        {
          "id": "d-ui-capacity",
          "question": "Who decides when work uses the UI?",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "o-ui-slot",
              "label": "The remote agent claims slots on the node with `ui-slot`",
              "rationale": "Only the remote agent knows when a long task starts validating, and node-side slots work for every client, including while Agent Teams is offline.",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-request-flags",
              "label": "The requester tags each request `/ui` or `/desktop`",
              "tradeoff": "The requester cannot know when a multi-hour task will touch the UI.",
              "adds": []
            }
          ],
          "choice": {
            "optionId": "o-ui-slot",
            "status": "decided"
          },
          "dependsOn": [],
          "affects": [],
          "basedOn": [
            "f-turn-meta"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-wake-owner",
          "question": "Who owns a sub session's self-wakes?",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "o-pool-wakes",
              "label": "Agent Teams",
              "rationale": "Wakes count against node limits, show beside the sub session, and are cancelled by newer messages.",
              "tradeoff": "Wakes fire only while Agent Teams runs; overdue ones fire at startup.",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-host-schedule",
              "label": "Agent Host scheduled chats",
              "tradeoff": "Host-started turns bypass the node's compute limit and the pool's status.",
              "adds": []
            }
          ],
          "choice": {
            "optionId": "o-pool-wakes",
            "status": "decided"
          },
          "dependsOn": [],
          "affects": []
        },
        {
          "id": "d-busy",
          "question": "What happens to a message sent to a session that is running a turn?",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "o-steer",
              "label": "It steers the running turn",
              "rationale": "A correction reaches the remote agent at its next step instead of after an hour of building; `/stop` remains the hard stop.",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-queue",
              "label": "It waits for the next turn",
              "tradeoff": "Corrections arrive too late to matter.",
              "adds": []
            }
          ],
          "choice": {
            "optionId": "o-steer",
            "status": "decided"
          },
          "dependsOn": [],
          "affects": []
        },
        {
          "id": "d-reply-audience",
          "question": "Who does a connector reply wake?",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "o-asker-only",
              "label": "Only the agent who asked",
              "rationale": "The lead still sees the result in the transcript, and the asker reports back as usual.",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-asker-and-lead",
              "label": "The asker and the lead",
              "tradeoff": "Every member's result would also wake the lead.",
              "adds": []
            }
          ],
          "choice": {
            "optionId": "o-asker-only",
            "status": "decided"
          },
          "dependsOn": [],
          "affects": []
        },
        {
          "id": "d-workflow-home",
          "question": "Where does the PR workflow live?",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "o-workspace-skill",
              "label": "A `remote-pr` skill in vscode-space",
              "rationale": "It builds on vscode-space's own skills and changes with that workspace without an Agent Teams release; other workspaces add their own workflow skills.",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-agent-teams",
              "label": "Built into Agent Teams",
              "tradeoff": "Ties a general pool to one workspace's process.",
              "adds": []
            }
          ],
          "choice": {
            "optionId": "o-workspace-skill",
            "status": "decided"
          },
          "dependsOn": [],
          "affects": []
        },
        {
          "id": "d-pr-close",
          "question": "May a remote session close a PR on its own?",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "o-confirm-close",
              "label": "Only with the requester's confirmation, unless the handoff allows it",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-close-freely",
              "label": "Yes",
              "tradeoff": "Work can be abandoned without anyone agreeing.",
              "adds": []
            }
          ],
          "choice": {
            "optionId": "o-confirm-close",
            "status": "decided"
          },
          "dependsOn": [],
          "affects": []
        },
        {
          "id": "d-finish",
          "question": "What does closing a sub session do?",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "o-finish-archives",
              "label": "Deletes it, archiving a remote one on its node",
              "rationale": "Archiving can be undone in VS Code; the owner closes a sub session once its task is done.",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-finish-keeps",
              "label": "Leaves it open",
              "tradeoff": "Finished sessions accumulate locally and on nodes.",
              "adds": []
            }
          ],
          "choice": {
            "optionId": "o-finish-archives",
            "status": "decided"
          },
          "dependsOn": [],
          "affects": []
        },
        {
          "id": "d-sub-shape",
          "question": "How do remote sub sessions fit into Agent Teams?",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "o-sub-provider",
              "label": "Workers run by a remote AHP provider",
              "rationale": "Local and remote sub sessions share one registry, toolset, and transcript view.",
              "tradeoff": "Re-plans the pool, and the AHP provider is proved first.",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-sub-connector",
              "label": "Keep the pool connector for remote work, and local sub sessions separately",
              "tradeoff": "Two registries and two toolsets, and remote work shows only as messages.",
              "adds": []
            }
          ],
          "choice": {
            "optionId": "o-sub-provider",
            "status": "decided"
          },
          "dependsOn": [],
          "affects": []
        },
        {
          "id": "d-sub-access",
          "question": "Who may message a sub session?",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "o-owner-only",
              "label": "Only its owning agent; the user can open and watch it",
              "rationale": "The owner stays the single reviewer, and the team's conversation stays quiet.",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-anyone",
              "label": "Any agent on the team, and the user",
              "tradeoff": "Instructions conflict, and the owner no longer sees everything its sub session is told.",
              "adds": []
            }
          ],
          "choice": {
            "optionId": "o-owner-only",
            "status": "decided"
          },
          "dependsOn": [],
          "affects": []
        },
        {
          "id": "d-native-subagents",
          "question": "How do sub sessions relate to the providers' own subagents?",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "o-native-alongside",
              "label": "Keep them for quick work within a turn, beside sub sessions",
              "rationale": "They already work and show as agent cards; sub sessions cover what they can't: other machines, follow-ups, and review.",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-native-wrap",
              "label": "Build sub sessions on the providers' subagents",
              "tradeoff": "They end with the parent's turn, run on the same machine, and differ by provider.",
              "adds": []
            }
          ],
          "choice": {
            "optionId": "o-native-alongside",
            "status": "decided"
          },
          "dependsOn": [],
          "affects": []
        },
        {
          "id": "d-node-registry",
          "question": "Where are agent nodes defined?",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "o-pool-connector-nodes",
              "label": "In a pool connector the owner adds nodes to",
              "rationale": "Nodes are added and edited where other outside links are, and the connector's link already holds each node's connection.",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-discovered-nodes",
              "label": "Discovered from labelled dev tunnels, each node describing itself",
              "tradeoff": "No manual entry, but node settings live on each node.",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-settings-nodes",
              "label": "Listed by hand in Settings",
              "tradeoff": "A second place to configure outside links.",
              "adds": []
            }
          ],
          "choice": {
            "optionId": "o-pool-connector-nodes",
            "status": "decided"
          },
          "dependsOn": [],
          "affects": []
        },
        {
          "id": "d-remote-security",
          "question": "How does Security cover what a remote sub session reads and changes outside its node?",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "o-open-review",
              "label": "For now, the node's own access, with every outcome reviewed",
              "rationale": "Remote sessions work with the node's access, the guard still reviews every external write the host holds, and each report lists the sub session's external writes for its owner to check against scope and context. Nothing large crosses the tunnel to be judged, and the sandbox with a node auditor can come later.",
              "tradeoff": "GitHub text the remote agent reads reaches its model unscreened until the node sandbox exists.",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-gateway",
              "label": "A sandboxed node whose only door is Agent Teams",
              "rationale": "The remote agent reads GitHub and the web only through Agent Teams client tools, which screen each result as a local session does.",
              "tradeoff": "Large content crosses the tunnel to be judged, and the node's own GitHub read path has to be removed first.",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-node-auditor",
              "label": "A sandboxed session judged by an auditor on its own node",
              "rationale": "Content is judged where it lives, with no large payloads crossing the tunnel.",
              "tradeoff": "Needs a session sandbox the host can't run yet; until then an auditor would read attacker content with tools it can't be denied.",
              "adds": []
            }
          ],
          "choice": {
            "optionId": "o-open-review",
            "status": "decided"
          },
          "dependsOn": [],
          "affects": [],
          "basedOn": [
            "f-ahp-approvals",
            "f-ahp-holds",
            "f-ahp-client-tools",
            "f-agent-sandbox"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-node-stack",
          "question": "What runs and exposes a node's agent host?",
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "o-tunnel-host",
              "label": "The owner's VS Code Tunnel Host, with the agent-node kit adding UI work",
              "rationale": "One tunnel and one agent host serve the editor, VS Code, and Agent Teams; the Tunnel Host gains idle updates, and the kit keeps `ui-slot` and the RDP hold.",
              "tradeoff": "Agent Teams relies on the tunnel's forwarded agent-host port, which VS Code could change.",
              "adds": []
            },
            {
              "id": "o-agent-node",
              "label": "The agent-node kit's own dev tunnel and agent host",
              "tradeoff": "A second tunnel and a second agent host on the same Copilot history, with a dev tunnel sign-in and a token file to copy.",
              "adds": []
            }
          ],
          "choice": {
            "optionId": "o-tunnel-host",
            "status": "decided"
          },
          "dependsOn": [],
          "affects": [],
          "basedOn": [
            "f-vscode-tunnel-ahp"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "questions",
      "title": "Experiments before building on them",
      "purpose": "Name the assumptions most likely to be wrong; each is settled on a node before the work that depends on it.",
      "collapsed": false,
      "kind": "questions",
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "q-tunnel-ws",
          "question": "Does a Bun WebSocket reach `code agent host` through a private dev tunnel with the tunnel authorization header and the AHP token, and reconnect after a drop?",
          "answerMethod": "run-experiment",
          "impact": "The package's `WebSocketTransport` cannot set headers, so a tunnel needs a small transport over Bun's `WebSocket`, which can.",
          "affects": [
            "r-remote-nodes",
            "r-remote-subscription"
          ],
          "answer": "Yes. From brchen-db4, Bun's `WebSocket` with an `X-Tunnel-Authorization: tunnel <connect token>` header (from `devtunnel token <id> --scopes connect`) and `?tkn=` reached brchen-db3's host at the port's `portUri`, negotiated 1.0.0, and ran an autopilot turn to completion. The first connection took 12.8 s while the tunnel relay warmed up; later fresh connections took 130 to 210 ms. Two more things the pool relies on: `resourceWrite` needs an existing folder, so the pool creates a session's scratch folder with `resourceMkdir`, which creates parents; and `listSessions` returns the node's whole Copilot history (49 sessions on brchen-db3), so the pool knows its own sessions from its threads rather than from the listing."
        },
        {
          "id": "q-ahp-client",
          "question": "Does `@microsoft/agent-host-protocol` 1.0.0 work with today's Insiders agent host, and can a turn's final answer be read reliably from chat state?",
          "answerMethod": "run-experiment",
          "affects": [
            "r-remote-provider",
            "r-sub-results"
          ],
          "answer": "Yes. On brchen-db4 the 1.0.0 client negotiated protocol 1.0.0 with Insiders agent host 944d79c over Bun's global WebSocket. A completed turn keeps its client-chosen ID, and in autopilot its last markdown part is a \"**Task completed:**\" summary that states the answer."
        },
        {
          "id": "q-steering",
          "question": "Does a steering message land in a running turn on today's Insiders host?",
          "answerMethod": "run-experiment",
          "affects": [
            "d-busy"
          ],
          "answer": "Yes. The host accepts it mid-turn and the agent applies it at its next step; when the turn ends first, here because its shell moved to background work, the agent applies it at the start of the next turn."
        },
        {
          "id": "q-resource-attach",
          "question": "Can a file written to the node with `resourceWrite` be attached to a turn?",
          "answerMethod": "run-experiment",
          "affects": [
            "r-files-in"
          ],
          "answer": "Yes. A file written with `resourceWrite` and attached as a resource attachment was read by the agent, which quoted its contents."
        },
        {
          "id": "q-client-tools",
          "question": "Is a client tool call made while the pool is disconnected answered after it reconnects?",
          "answerMethod": "run-experiment",
          "affects": [
            "r-remote-tools"
          ],
          "answer": "Yes, for a 20-second disconnect: the host kept the client's tools and the running call, and completing it after reconnecting with the same client ID finished the turn. The protocol lets a host time out a call whose client stays away, so a longer outage can fail it."
        },
        {
          "id": "q-background-wake",
          "question": "When a session's background work ends after its turn, does the host start a turn by itself, or must Agent Teams wake the session?",
          "answerMethod": "run-experiment",
          "affects": [
            "r-sub-results",
            "r-sub-wakes"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "q-ui-concurrency",
          "question": "Do five Playwright-driven VS Code windows and a browser run on one node without interfering, and what do they cost in CPU and memory?",
          "answerMethod": "run-experiment",
          "affects": [
            "r-ui-slot"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "q-rdp-console",
          "question": "After an RDP disconnect moves the session to the console, do Playwright work and real mouse and keyboard input both keep working, and does a company screen-lock policy apply on brchen-db3?",
          "answerMethod": "run-experiment",
          "impact": "If brchen-db3 is a physical PC rather than a cloud VM, leaving its console unlocked needs a different approach.",
          "affects": [
            "r-rdp-hold"
          ],
          "answer": "Yes, with two kit fixes. On brchen-db3 the connect task took the human hold on reconnect and the disconnect task released it and moved the session to the console with `tscon`, both exiting 0. The console stayed unlocked, but at 1024x768. A pool session then took a desktop hold and ran headed Edge under Playwright: page screenshots and full-desktop captures both worked and streamed back over AHP within a second or two. The Edge window opened behind the window already in front, because Windows keeps a background process from taking the foreground, so the kit gained `ui-slot focus`, which brought it forward by attaching to the foreground window's input; real keyboard input and a real mouse click then reached the page. A session set the console to 1920x1080 the same way the disconnect task now does. Not yet tested: whether a screen-lock policy locks the console after a long idle."
        },
        {
          "id": "q-signins",
          "question": "Does a copied Edge profile stay signed into Lens, can local VS Code complete MCP sign-in for a pool-created session, and is device-code `az login` allowed?",
          "answerMethod": "run-experiment",
          "affects": [
            "r-setup-record",
            "r-human-needed"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "q-root-worktree",
          "question": "Does a session started at the vscode-space root on brchen-db3 create its own worktree under `vscode.worktrees/` when given a child-repo change?",
          "answerMethod": "run-experiment",
          "affects": [
            "pr-workflow",
            "r-placement"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "q-ahp-provider",
          "question": "Can a provider project a node's AHP turns into Agent Teams session events, replay them from the node's history, offer Agent Teams tools as AHP client tools, and pass their calls through Security's tool checks?",
          "answerMethod": "run-experiment",
          "impact": "If projection or replay loses too much, remote sub sessions fall back to reporting results without transcripts.",
          "affects": [
            "r-remote-provider",
            "r-remote-tools",
            "d-sub-shape"
          ],
          "answer": "Yes for transcripts and Agent Teams tools, and partly for Security. On brchen-db3 a probe projected a live autopilot turn's chat actions into session events, and a fresh connection's history replayed into the identical transcript, shell output included. An Agent Teams tool offered as a client tool ran through the guard's review and screen and returned its result to the model. The guard refused a write the host held for confirmation, but the host ran reads without asking and can't let a client rewrite native results (f-ahp-approvals), which d-remote-security settles."
        },
        {
          "id": "q-node-read-path",
          "question": "Can a pool session lose the node's own GitHub read path, through the `gh` CLI and the GitHub MCP server, while `git push` keeps working and the owner's own sessions on the same machine keep theirs?",
          "answerMethod": "run-experiment",
          "impact": "Without it, the later node sandbox (r-node-sandbox) is advisory: GitHub text a remote agent reads itself reaches its model unscreened.",
          "affects": [
            "r-node-sandbox"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "plan",
      "title": "Plan",
      "purpose": "Order the work so each step leaves the product working and the riskiest assumptions are tested before the pool depends on them.",
      "collapsed": false,
      "kind": "plan",
      "fields": [],
      "phases": [
        {
          "id": "phase-reply",
          "title": "Reply routing",
          "steps": [
            {
              "id": "s-reply-to",
              "title": "Route connector replies to the agent who asked",
              "doneWhen": "A connector event whose `replyTo` names an agent's message wakes that agent and shows it as the transcript addressee; events without it, or answering the user, wake the lead; model, database, and server tests cover each case and pass.",
              "status": "complete",
              "implements": [
                "r-reply-to-wire",
                "r-asked-by",
                "r-audience",
                "r-guidance",
                "d-reply-audience"
              ],
              "values": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "s-bryanbot-reply-to",
              "title": "Send `replyTo` from BryanBot",
              "doneWhen": "Every BryanBot event answering an Agent Teams message carries that message's ID, and BryanBot's connector tests pass.",
              "status": "complete",
              "implements": [
                "r-bryanbot-reply-to"
              ],
              "values": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "phase-node",
          "title": "Node kit",
          "steps": [
            {
              "id": "s-node-host",
              "title": "Serve the Insiders agent host on brchen-db3",
              "doneWhen": "After logon, brchen-db3's Tunnel Host serves the editor and the agent host through one owner-only VS Code tunnel, the agent-node kit's own dev tunnel and agent host are gone, and an idle maintenance run updates the CLI and workspaces, then restarts the tunnel.",
              "status": "in-progress",
              "implements": [
                "r-node-host",
                "r-node-maintenance",
                "r-node-setup-script",
                "d-quality",
                "d-node-stack"
              ],
              "values": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "s-ui-slot",
              "title": "Build `ui-slot` and the RDP hold",
              "doneWhen": "Of six concurrent `ui-slot run` calls, five run and one waits; a desktop hold waits for all slots and blocks new ones; killing a holder frees its slot; an RDP connect pauses desktop holds and a disconnect returns the session to the console unlocked.",
              "status": "complete",
              "implements": [
                "r-ui-slot",
                "r-rdp-hold",
                "d-ui-capacity"
              ],
              "values": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "s-node-skill",
              "title": "Install the agent-node skill and setup record",
              "doneWhen": "A new session on the node finds the `agent-node` skill and records a confirmed setup without secrets.",
              "status": "in-progress",
              "implements": [
                "r-node-skill",
                "r-setup-record"
              ],
              "values": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "phase-sub",
          "title": "Sub sessions",
          "steps": [
            {
              "id": "s-sub-tools",
              "title": "Give every agent one sub-session toolset",
              "doneWhen": "A team agent creates a local sub session from a brief, messages it, lists it, and closes it; a sixth open sub session is refused; another agent can't message it; and deleting the agent deletes it.",
              "status": "complete",
              "implements": [
                "r-sub-owner",
                "r-sub-tools",
                "r-sub-brief",
                "r-sub-limits",
                "r-sub-access",
                "d-sub-access",
                "r-sub-guidance",
                "d-native-subagents"
              ],
              "values": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "s-sub-results",
              "title": "Report each finished turn to the owner",
              "doneWhen": "Each finished turn wakes the owner once with its answer and evidence, `NO_UPDATE` wakes no one, and a `wake_after` starts a later turn that a newer message cancels.",
              "status": "complete",
              "implements": [
                "r-sub-results",
                "r-sub-wakes",
                "x-sub-flow",
                "d-wake-owner",
                "d-busy"
              ],
              "values": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "s-sub-view",
              "title": "Show each agent's sub sessions and close the forgotten ones",
              "doneWhen": "The team pane lists each agent's open sub sessions; an idle reported sub session reminds its owner once; and deleting a team closes its members' sub sessions.",
              "status": "complete",
              "implements": [
                "r-sub-view",
                "r-sub-cleanup",
                "d-finish"
              ],
              "values": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "s-outcome-review",
              "title": "Hand each owner its sub session's external writes",
              "doneWhen": "A sub session that pushes a branch and comments on an issue reports both writes to its owner, who checks them against the brief; a report with no external writes lists none; and the same holds for a remote sub session.",
              "status": "in-progress",
              "implements": [
                "r-outcome-review",
                "d-remote-security"
              ],
              "values": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "phase-remote",
          "title": "Remote sub sessions",
          "steps": [
            {
              "id": "s-ahp-probe",
              "title": "Prove an AHP provider against brchen-db3",
              "doneWhen": "A probe projects a real brchen-db3 turn into session events, replays it from the node's history, and calls an Agent Teams tool offered as an AHP client tool through Security's checks, answering q-ahp-provider.",
              "status": "complete",
              "implements": [
                "d-sub-shape"
              ],
              "values": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "s-remote-provider",
              "title": "Run sub sessions on a node through the remote provider",
              "doneWhen": "A sub session created for brchen-db3's pool runs there with its transcript and history in Agent Teams; a retried create makes one session; messages and steering reach it; attachments reach the node; and mentioning the pool addresses nothing.",
              "implements": [
                "r-remote-provider",
                "r-remote-nodes",
                "r-idempotent",
                "r-remote-subscription",
                "r-files-in",
                "d-runtime",
                "d-path",
                "r-pool-location",
                "d-node-registry"
              ],
              "values": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "s-remote-placement",
              "title": "Place remote sub sessions on nodes",
              "doneWhen": "A sub session for a team working in a node's repository starts in that folder, another starts in a scratch folder, a draining or full node takes none, and waiting requests start in arrival order.",
              "implements": [
                "r-placement"
              ],
              "values": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "s-remote-tools",
              "title": "Give remote sub sessions their node tools",
              "doneWhen": "A remote sub session sets its status, schedules a wake, and raises a human request that the user's answer resumes, and each node's slot use shows in Settings.",
              "implements": [
                "r-remote-tools",
                "r-human-needed",
                "r-status"
              ],
              "values": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "phase-workflow",
          "title": "PR workflow",
          "steps": [
            {
              "id": "s-remote-pr",
              "title": "Write and dogfood the `remote-pr` skill",
              "doneWhen": "One real microsoft/vscode fix goes from a team handoff through a remote session's draft PR, CI, review, merge, and worktree cleanup, and the requester hears only milestones.",
              "implements": [
                "pr-workflow",
                "d-workflow-home",
                "d-pr-close"
              ],
              "values": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "phase-later",
          "title": "Later",
          "steps": [
            {
              "id": "s-files-out",
              "title": "Return files from remote sessions",
              "doneWhen": "A remote session's screenshot arrives as a thread attachment and its video as a link.",
              "implements": [
                "r-files-out"
              ],
              "values": {}
            },
            {
              "id": "s-node-sandbox",
              "title": "Sandbox remote sessions behind a node auditor",
              "doneWhen": "A remote session on brchen-db3 reads GitHub and the web only through its node's auditor, which screens each result on the node, while the owner's own sessions there keep their access.",
              "implements": [
                "r-node-sandbox"
              ],
              "values": {}
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
